92186366520 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366520 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5fb7b1df-59af-5c98-b200-8e2db9ace225" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366521 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -6=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366521 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366521 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "780e1a23-2b19-569c-80de-ba05aa572a33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366522 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\lambda }^{3}+4{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +10=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366522 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366522 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1e23239c-8a66-52b7-8ebb-bb679392ff42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366523 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -6=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366523 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366523 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d9d592de-a8e2-59d7-af6e-a3bf5bfd67b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366524 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -10=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366524 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366524 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "023efe6a-1a85-50a5-b630-83708ee21893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376089 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454471 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186376089 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454472 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186376089 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454473 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186376089 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376089 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376089 #edb/ref 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<x+3},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=x+3}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(-2,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(-2,1)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(-2,3)$ is an interior point of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186376089 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7257b8a6-8ea5-5027-98d4-5845f1b59c79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186376089 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=x+3.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7a/7a23cbece856b7b842d5907665af5ea593a06faea79283b1be13fc4382e0bf26.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-2,1)$ and $Q(-2,3)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/82/82c921570bbc1ce700db5472871048c14c67df7d6895962f87454c70fc1ce192.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,1)$ satisfy $y=x+3.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-2,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<x+3.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186